WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.120
According to author David Robson,

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:04.200
we're not always as smart
as we think we are.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.440
Psychological research suggests
that greater intelligence,

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:11.520
education and expertise might
actually amplify our errors.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:22.240
Consider this question.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:27.360
How many animals of each species
did Moses take on the ark?

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.600
The answer of course is zero.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:37.640
But many intelligent people
get this wrong.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:41.920
They are cognitive misers, they are
capable of intelligent reasoning

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.000
but they don't apply
that brain power effectively,

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:48.160
instead relying too much
on their gut feelings.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:51.000
Cognitive miserliness
can cause us to be swayed

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:53.960
by irrelevant information
and our own feelings.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:56.760
For example, leading us
to poor financial decisions

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:57.640
when buying a house.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.760
It can also explain why
apparently intelligent people

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.600
can fall for fake news if they
rely too much on the gist

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:05.520
rather than the details
of a statement.

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:12.960
Sometimes, thanks to the
emotional pull of an argument,

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.080
we think in a very one-sided manner.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:20.640
So Arthur Conan Doyle is the perfect
example of motivated reasoning.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:23.480
Now he was obviously
an incredibly intelligent man -

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:27.440
he was a doctor and also wrote
all of the Sherlock Holmes books

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.600
where he really shows
a very clear understanding

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:32.280
of what logical
deduction should be.

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:34.320
But in his own private life,

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:37.000
Arthur Conan Doyle
was not nearly so rational.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:41.760
He had a very strong
emotional belief in spiritualism

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:44.640
and often visited fraudulent mediums.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:46.680
Now, Arthur Conan Doyle's friends

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:49.560
which included
Harry Houdini the illusionist,

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.200
often tried to persuade him
that he was wrong

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:53.880
and to show him the evidence

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:56.600
that he was being scammed
by these people,

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:59.920
but Arthur Conan Doyle
just didn't believe their arguments.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.760
So for instance he would
try to bring in

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:06.840
the latest physics on the
electromagnetic field

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:09.120
to explain how fairies might exist

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.440
but just appeared
in another wavelength.

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:13.320
You may not believe in fairies

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:16.920
but motivated reasoning can lead
to polarized political views.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:20.160
It may also allow you to rationalise
poor business ventures

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:21.240
or a failing love affair.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:28.480
You may hope that education
or professional expertise

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:29.800
would protect you from error

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:31.720
but these can also backfire.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:33.520
After years of experience in a job,

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.600
experts for instance
may begin to act on auto-pilot

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:40.680
and that automatic decision-making
can sometimes miss vital information.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:45.840
This may explain
a terrible case from 2004

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:49.160
when a man called Brandon Mayfield

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:52.800
was accused of conducting
the Madrid bombings.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:57.880
Now Brandon Mayfield had not
left the USA during that time

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.800
and yet the FBI's
fingerprint examiners

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:02.760
still accused him of the crime.

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:06.560
The Spanish police
had found a fingerprint

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:09.440
on a plastic bag
near the scene of the crime.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.920
Now the FBI had put that
into their computers

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:15.600
and they had found
about 20 candidates

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:17.400
who might have made the match.

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:19.280
And when they looked
at Brandon Mayfield's,

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:21.480
they were sure
that they were identical.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:23.240
Yet when you look at the fingerprints

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:25.680
there are actually some very
important differences

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:27.400
that they had completely missed

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.440
but thanks to the curse of expertise

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:32.200
they were susceptible
to confirmation bias.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:34.640
So they only saw
the bits that matched

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:37.320
and were completely blind
to the bits that didn't match.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:44.520
Sometimes smart people
can act stupidly

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:46.120
thanks to the people
who are around us.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.680
A sense of conflict
and competition within a group

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:50.800
can actually reduce
each team member's

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:52.880
problem-solving skills
and creativity.

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:55.520
Even just one or two
arrogant team members

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:57.840
can completely ruin the group dynamic

00:03:57.920 --> 00:03:59.880
and reduce the performance
of the whole team.

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:02.800
One study found that people's
individual IQ levels

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.880
actually drop when they feel
in competition with others.

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:10.960
Just think of Iceland's victory in
the 2016 Euro Championships.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:15.600
Now Iceland is a tiny country
compared to the rest of Europe,

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:17.960
it really doesn't have
a huge pool of talent,

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:20.920
and yet they performed
extraordinarily well

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:24.320
and in fact they humiliated
the England football team

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:28.480
by defeating them despite
all of our really top tier players.

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:31.400
Now this is an example
of the too-much-talent-effect.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:34.680
Because England with
so many star players

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:38.320
really struggled to have
a cohesive group dynamic,

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:40.880
there were too many egos
vying for attention.

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:44.320
Luckily there are things that you can
do to avoid these thinking traps.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:46.880
For example, you can try
arguing against yourself.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:49.320
Consider your initial gut reaction

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:52.760
and then deliberately try to think
from the opposite point of view.

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:55.920
Another technique you can
use is called self-distancing.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:57.480
Step out of your shoes for a second

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:00.520
and think about your issue
from an outside perspective.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:02.520
Or you might try mental time travel.

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:05.280
Imagine yourself in a month
or a year's time,

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:06.440
looking back at your decision.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:10.000
Lastly, why not try fine-tuning
your emotional awareness.

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:13.480
Being able to label our feelings
helps us to control them.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:16.080
Various studies have shown
that this simple technique

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:18.320
has actually reduced
emotional responses

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:20.120
and made people less biased.

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:27.880
